    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on some walnut cabinet joinery and I'm having a bit of an issue. I'm getting some slight splintering at the tips of the pins when I'm cutting my dovetails. The grain is relatively straight, but it's still chipping. Any advice on chisel maintenance or how to better manage the grain to prevent this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Make sure your chisels are sharp; dull tools are much more likely to cause splintering. A sharp chisel makes clean, precise cuts. Additionally, apply gentle, even pressure while cutting, and remember to always chisel away from your body. Finally, make sure your work surface is stable, and use a backing board if necessary to support the wood and minimize splintering. If the grain still causes issues, you may consider changing wood species with a more consistent grain or using predrilled holes to reduce tear-out on the grain when inserting your pins.",
      "source": "target",
