    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. I've tried honing the chisels to a mirror finish, so I'm pretty sure the edge is fine. It almost feels like even with a sharp tool, the wood is resisting or something. Do you think it could be the moisture content in the walnut? Or maybe the way I'm approaching the angle of the cut? I'm trying to avoid any tear-out at all costs.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Moisture content can certainly affect cutting, though 12-15% moisture content should be ideal for hand tools. Ensure the wood is not too dry or overly moist. Consider acclimatizing the wood to your shop to prevent warping during the work. As for the angle of the cut, ensure it's not too aggressive – it should taper slightly outwards, and keep it consistent across cuts. Try to cut at right angles to trunk and heart, running parallel with the grain. Using sharp, fresh-dipped brass scrapers can help to clean up the edges and relieve some split tendencies. Also, immerse your chisel blades in a honing steel dipped in mineral oil shortly before use – sometimes extra lubrication can help ease the cutting action.",
